# 3. Why doesn't OBO — On-Behalf-Of — suffice?

OBO is useful.

OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange explicitly supports delegation/impersonation scenarios. But OBO primarily helps propagate or exchange authorization context.

The conceptual model is:

```text
User
  │
  ▼
Agent A
  │
  │ OBO
  ▼
Agent B
  │
  ▼
API
```

The problem with autonomous agents is that the chain can become:

```text
Human
  ↓
Agent A
  ↓
Agent B
  ↓
Agent C
  ↓
MCP
  ↓
Tool
  ↓
External API
```

The question becomes:

> **Does the final authorization token adequately represent the complete reasoning and delegation context?**

Not necessarily.

Consider:

```text
Human
  │
  │ "Create a campaign"
  ▼
Campaign Agent
  │
  │ delegates
  ▼
Identity Agent
  │
  │ delegates
  ▼
Audience Agent
  │
  │ calls
  ▼
Customer Graph
```

OBO can establish:

```text
Agent C → acting on behalf of User
```

But your governance layer wants:

```text
Agent C
 ├── originating human
 ├── parent agent
 ├── delegation reason
 ├── original purpose
 ├── capabilities inherited
 ├── capabilities narrowed
 ├── data classification
 ├── confidence
 ├── policy decision
 ├── expiration
 └── complete provenance
```

That's the difference between:

**delegated authorization**

and

**governed delegation.**

