## One important correction to your "NemoClaw → OpenShell" story

I would **not describe NemoClaw as simply the previous name for OpenShell**.

The current NVIDIA documentation describes **OpenShell as the general-purpose sandbox runtime and policy platform**, while **NemoClaw is an opinionated stack/reference setup built on OpenShell** for supported agent runtimes. ([NVIDIA Docs][5])

So your slide should say:

```text
OpenShell
   │
   │ general-purpose runtime
   ▼
Sandbox + Gateway + Supervisor + Policy

NemoClaw
   │
   │ opinionated integration/reference stack
   ▼
Agent runtime + configuration + blueprint
   +
OpenShell
```

That distinction will make your technical explanation much more credible.

### The one-line thesis for your demo

> **OBO tells us who an agent is acting on behalf of; identity governance determines what that delegated agent is allowed to become and do; OpenShell provides the runtime boundary that makes those decisions enforceable.**

That gives you a clean separation between **IAM/OBO**, **your agent-governance layer**, and **OpenShell runtime security**.

